 "texts": {
  "fields": [
   {
    "name": "demonstration_reference",
    "description": "At the video cycle, store an ordered pair of frame labels: the last frame showing the uncovered colored cubes, followed by the first frame showing the settled containers covering them. Retain this pair throughout execution. These images preserve the color-to-container correspondence, including targets obscured by the hand later. e.g. [\"V16\", \"V32\"]",
    "annotation": "At the video cycle, store an ordered pair of frame labels: the last frame showing the uncovered colored cubes, followed by the first frame showing the settled containers covering them. Retain this pair throughout execution. These images preserve the color-to-container correspondence, including targets obscured by the hand later. e.g. [\"V16\", \"V32\"]",
    "value_type": "keyframe"
   },
   {
    "name": "completed_lifts",
    "description": "Write zero at the video cycle. At an act cycle, increment the incoming count once only if this cycle's motion images show the closed hand lifting the next requested container from near z=0.063 m to near z=0.20 m, with the container moving with the hand and its cube exposed. A stationary elevated grasp or a downward motion is not another lift. Otherwise copy the incoming count, including after release and retreat. e.g. 1",
    "annotation": "Initialize to zero at the video cycle. Increment once at the act cycle showing a requested container successfully raised from grasp height to approximately z=0.20 m while held closed, exposing its cube. Count requested containers in instruction order. Do not increment on approach, grasp closure, lowering, release, or retreat. Retain the count otherwise. e.g. 1",
    "value_type": "count"
   }
  ],
  "action": "At the video cycle write memory only. Execution begins with an open hand high above the table. Read the requested colors in instruction order and use demonstration_reference to associate each with its covering container. Match the uncovered cube locations to the covering events and settled arrangement, not to arbitrary container ordering; ignore extra containers. Use the current images to localize the corresponding container. Front-image right is positive world y.\n\nLocalize a resting container by reconstructing its upper horizontal square face on the world z=0.05 m plane. Identify that face's four outer corners, excluding vertical side faces and the lower rim; do not use the whole silhouette or the grasp TCP height as the reconstruction plane. For each corner pixel p=(u,v,1), compute the camera center C=-R^T t and world ray d=R^T K^{-1}p using the view's calibration. Intersect the ray with the face plane: X=C+((0.05-C_z)/d_z)d. Average the four reconstructed corners to obtain the grasp-axis x,y, and recover yaw from the reconstructed square edges. If corners are obscured, fit the square to visible top-face edges and check against the other calibrated view rather than averaging the silhouette. Use the current wrist-camera extrinsics for the wrist view. Command the open-hand approach at this reconstructed x,y and z=0.20 m. Use a downward hand, roll=3.1416 and pitch=0, with yaw aligned to the container's square sides, choosing the equivalent yaw closest to zero. Container grasp TCP height is approximately 0.0633 m. Approach and lift height is 0.20 m. Re-estimate the actual container center rather than using the achieved TCP x,y blindly: approach motions can undershoot by more than a centimetre.\n\nApply these state-dependent rules, using completed_lifts updated from the motion just observed:\n\nIf the hand is closed around a container at low grasp height, lift that container to z=0.20 with the gripper closed. Center the lift over the container's current x,y, preserving its aligned yaw. This rule takes precedence over selecting another target.\n\nIf a completed requested lift leaves a container held at z approximately 0.20 and further requested colors remain, lower it vertically at the current TCP x,y to z=0.070 and open the gripper at arrival; preserve the current orientation. Do not carry it to the next target or drop it from approach height.\n\nIf the gripper is open near z=0.070 following that replacement, retreat vertically at the current x,y to z=0.146 with the gripper open and orientation preserved. The next target is not approached laterally until this clearance is reached.\n\nOtherwise, with an empty open hand, select requested color number completed_lifts+1. If not already above that container at approximately z=0.20, move to its center x,y at z=0.20 with aligned downward orientation and the gripper open. This includes the initial high pose and the post-release clearance pose. If already above the selected container at approach height, descend to its center x,y at z=0.0633 and close at arrival. At approach height, reconstruct the same top face again using the current calibrated wrist image, checked against the front view, to correct lateral undershoot. Descend directly to the reconstructed x,y at the grasp TCP height and close, without inserting an extra approach waypoint. The z=0.05 m reconstruction plane is distinct from the z=0.0633 m grasp TCP height.\n\nAfter the final requested lift, keep holding the container; do not replace it. If another cycle arrives despite completion, wait briefly with the grasp unchanged.\n\nFor an ambiguous view, use the retained demonstration images and current wrist image together; do not guess a different container from proximity alone. If localization remains uncertain, make a safe open-hand upward clearance move before reassessing, or briefly wait if holding an object. Never issue an identical-pose waypoint; any necessary gripper-only change must include at least 0.002 m of safe motion. If an action executed no steps, do not infer progress or increment the lift count."
